=doc
# Doc blocks

A line that starts with `=` and a letter, between top-level forms, begins
a doc block, and a line `=cut` ends it (D156, D164). The compiler skips
it, so this file compiles as if it weren't there.
=cut

; This file reads itself: with (:read :source) it gets its forms and doc
; blocks in order, each with the line it starts on; with (:read :sexp),
; just its forms.

(let known '(defun let pprint map doc))

=doc
## What it prints

For a doc block, its line and its first line of text; for a form, its
line and its head.
=cut

(defun outline (seen)
    (recv
        ((:open f)          (send f (list :read :source)) (outline seen))
        ((:form f v line)   (outline (cons (list line (car v)) seen)))
        ((:doc f text line) (outline (cons (list line :doc (car (str-split text "\n"))) seen)))
        ((:eof f)           (reverse seen))))

(defun forms (heads)
    (recv
        ((:open f)   (send f (list :read :sexp)) (forms heads))
        ((:sexp f v) (forms (cons (car v) heads)))
        ((:eof f)    (reverse heads))))

(map pprint (cadr (join (connect :fs/read "t/184-doc-blocks.slight" (outline ())))))
(pprint (join (connect :fs/read "t/184-doc-blocks.slight" (forms ()))))

; inside a form, a line =doc is a name like any other
(pprint '(before
=doc
after))

; sexp/parse skips doc blocks as space, but not after a quote, which
; waits for its datum; and says where a bad one goes wrong: an unknown
; kind, a =cut with no block, more after =doc
(pprint (sexp/parse "=doc\nabout it\n=cut\n(data)\n=doc\nafter it"))
(pprint (sexp/parse "'\n=doc\n"))
(pprint (sexp/parse "=example\n(+ 1 2)\n=cut\n"))
(pprint (sexp/parse "(data)\n=cut\n"))
(pprint (sexp/parse "=doc Title\n=cut\n(data)"))
(pprint (sexp/parse "=dog\n=cut\n(data)"))
(pprint (sexp/parse "(data) =doc"))

=doc
The last block has no =cut: the end of the file ends it.
